1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,238
Every evening a bank builds a wall: its ninety-nine percent value at risk,

2
00:00:04,338 --> 00:00:07,581
a loss it expects to exceed on only one day in a hundred.

3
00:00:07,681 --> 00:00:10,923
Here is that wall for the S and P five hundred, built the

4
00:00:11,023 --> 00:00:15,144
textbook way, and nine years of losses rolling in. The promise was about

5
00:00:15,244 --> 00:00:18,838
twenty-three days over the wall. The sea delivered fifty-three.

6
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:23,197
To build the wall we need two things: how rough the

7
00:00:23,297 --> 00:00:26,333
sea is today, and how tall its rare waves can be.

8
00:00:27,433 --> 00:00:31,161
First, how rough is the sea? A daily return sees only two

9
00:00:31,261 --> 00:00:35,123
prices, the open and the close. Here are two days that both

10
00:00:35,223 --> 00:00:38,615
end up half a percent: a calm one, and a stormy one.

11
00:00:38,715 --> 00:00:42,980
Their daily returns, and so their squared returns, are identical.

12
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:46,542
Now sum the squares of the tiny one-minute moves as

13
00:00:46,642 --> 00:00:50,328
the day goes on. The calm day collects almost nothing;

14
00:00:50,428 --> 00:00:54,465
the stormy one collects twenty-five times more. That sum is

15
00:00:54,565 --> 00:00:59,162
realized volatility: a direct, model-free reading of the day's sea.

16
00:01:00,233 --> 00:01:04,510
Most days a bank has only closing prices. RiskMetrics then measures the

17
00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:08,949
sea from footprints: each day's squared return is pressed into the sand,

18
00:01:09,049 --> 00:01:12,833
and every evening all the older prints fade by six percent. The

19
00:01:12,933 --> 00:01:15,545
weight of a day halves in about eleven days.

20
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:22,621
February twenty eighteen: a few deep prints, and tomorrow's volatility jumps at

21
00:01:22,721 --> 00:01:27,321
once. Then the walk goes on, and the deep prints slowly fade.

22
00:01:27,421 --> 00:01:31,945
There is no constant, so no level to return to: only memory.

23
00:01:33,033 --> 00:01:38,076
Is the wall too low because RiskMetrics misjudges the roughness? No. Divide

24
00:01:38,176 --> 00:01:42,190
